STEP 1 — Prepare a Download Folder on Ubuntu
On your Ubuntu machine with internet:
mkdir flask_offline
cd flask_offline

This keeps everything clean and easy to transfer.

STEP 2 — Download Flask and All Dependencies (No Install Yet)
Run this:
pip3 download flask

This will download:

Flask
Werkzeug
Jinja2
Click
itsdangerous

All as *.whl (wheel) files.
If you want to confirm:
ls

You should see something like:
Flask-2.2.5-py3-none-any.whl
Werkzeug-2.2.3-py3-none-any.whl
Jinja2-3.1.2-py3-none-any.whl
Click-8.1.3-py3-none-any.whl
itsdangerous-2.1.2-py3-none-any.whl

These files represent "offline Flask."

STEP 3 — Transfer Files to the Debian Host
Copy the flask_offline folder to your Debian machine using:

USB
SCP
rsync
shared folder
whatever your environment allows

For example, using SCP from Ubuntu:
scp -r flask_offline youruser@<debian-ip>:/home/youruser/

Or manually via USB.

STEP 4 — Install Flask Offline on Debian
On the Debian machine:
cd ~/flask_offline
pip3 install *.whl

If your Debian system requires sudo:
sudo pip3 install *.whl

This installs Flask without needing internet.

STEP 5 — Verify the Installation
Run:
python3 -c "import flask; print('Flask installed successfully')"

If you see:
Flask installed successfully

then you're ready to run the HVAC server.
